%I A187879 #20 Jul 26 2026 16:00:38
%S A187879 1,3,6561,7625597484987,3433683820292512484657849089281,
%T A187879 436673502879206784130402698570834024654748577491697818855443
%N A187879 a(n) = 3^(n^3).
%C A187879 Next term (11433811...77770721) has 104 digits.
%C A187879 From _Peter Bala_, Jul 24 2026: (Start)
%C A187879 This sequence is an example of a polynomial recursive sequence as defined by Cadilhac et al: a(n+1) = P(a(n), b(n), c(n)), b(n+1) = Q(a(n), b(n), c(n)) and c(n+1) = R(a(n), b(n), c(n)) with a(0) = b(0) = c(0) = 1, where P(x, y, z) = 3*x*y, Q(x, y, z) = 729*y*z and R(x, y, z) = 729*z
%C A187879 For prime p, the sequence obtained by reducing a(n) modulo p is ultimately periodic. For example, reducing the sequence modulo 17 gives the sequence [1, 3, 16, 7, 1, 12, 16, 11, 1, 14, 16, 10, 1, 5, 16, 6,  1, 3, 16, 7, 1, 12, 16, 11, 1, 14, 16, 10, 1, 5, 16, 6,  1, 3, 16, 7, 1, 12, 16, 11, 1, 14, 16, 10, 1, 5, 16, 6,  1, 3, 16, 11, ...], with period 16 starting at n = 0. (End)
%H A187879 Vincenzo Librandi, <a href="/A187879/b187879.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..12</a>
%H A187879 Michaël Cadilhac, Filip Mazowiecki, Charles Paperman, Michał Pilipczuk, and Géraud Sénizergues, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.08630">On polynomial recursive sequences</a>, arXiv:2002.08630 [cs.FL], 2020.
%F A187879 a(n+1)^3*a(n+3) - 729*a(n)*a(n+2)^3 = 0 for n >= 0. - _Peter Bala_, Jul 24 2026
